[[:db/retract 17592186146564 :answer-field/choices 17592186146565] [:db/retract 17592186146564 :answer-field/choices 17592186146566] [:db/retract 17592186146564 :answer-field/choices 17592186146567] [:db/retract 17592186146564 :answer-field/choices 17592186146568] [:db/retract 17592186146564 :answer-field/choices 17592186146569] {:db/id 17592186147345 :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\nI. $y'' + 81y = 0$\n\nII. $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n\nIII. $y'' - 81y = 0$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3951f87d-bb0f-5303-830e-d70693674542"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147347 :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 9 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n\nII. $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n\nIII. $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147431 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-58a73fa1-b7ae-5890-859a-ca230c9a8f1d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a1d55e9e-973d-5821-a2d2-76fe96bd1d7d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147349 :question/problem "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n\nII. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n\nIII. The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)},$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148135 :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=64\\,⟹\\,\\omega =8.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{8}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186146564 :answer-field/correct "answer-e52d9a63-cb05-593f-8c88-406ceb09fed1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4e0dfdc-13d0-53f8-905f-062768c52829" "answer-474cbbae-5bb2-50b5-adf4-ee5063eb2c40" "answer-e52d9a63-cb05-593f-8c88-406ceb09fed1" "answer-c213ebe9-776a-591e-b982-8a9a80a51a15" "answer-114d6b8f-6471-5003-8bc6-bf96f5a4a260"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4e0dfdc-13d0-53f8-905f-062768c52829" :answer/id #uuid "f4e0dfdc-13d0-53f8-905f-062768c52829" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-474cbbae-5bb2-50b5-adf4-ee5063eb2c40" :answer/id #uuid "474cbbae-5bb2-50b5-adf4-ee5063eb2c40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e52d9a63-cb05-593f-8c88-406ceb09fed1" :answer/id #uuid "e52d9a63-cb05-593f-8c88-406ceb09fed1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c213ebe9-776a-591e-b982-8a9a80a51a15" :answer/id #uuid "c213ebe9-776a-591e-b982-8a9a80a51a15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-114d6b8f-6471-5003-8bc6-bf96f5a4a260" :answer/id #uuid "114d6b8f-6471-5003-8bc6-bf96f5a4a260" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3951f87d-bb0f-5303-830e-d70693674542" :question/id #uuid "3951f87d-bb0f-5303-830e-d70693674542" :question/math-academy-id "q-94279" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)$\n\nTherefore, only statement III could be used to represent  $y(t).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf16abc1-c544-5419-8b7c-43b624566e09"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf16abc1-c544-5419-8b7c-43b624566e09" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf16abc1-c544-5419-8b7c-43b624566e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4cf31f8d-0b9b-54c4-9952-e5cce2f4eb4d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ffa1a9f9-35e4-54a6-80a6-ff1b93c42bad" "answer-ec54620d-42a6-5d21-824a-bb0be4ebb61d" "answer-8411bef0-d8a8-527d-a004-2dd2d0cf6447" "answer-d83fc18f-3328-5871-b2d1-03511b17539e" "answer-4cf31f8d-0b9b-54c4-9952-e5cce2f4eb4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffa1a9f9-35e4-54a6-80a6-ff1b93c42bad" :answer/id #uuid "ffa1a9f9-35e4-54a6-80a6-ff1b93c42bad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec54620d-42a6-5d21-824a-bb0be4ebb61d" :answer/id #uuid "ec54620d-42a6-5d21-824a-bb0be4ebb61d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8411bef0-d8a8-527d-a004-2dd2d0cf6447" :answer/id #uuid "8411bef0-d8a8-527d-a004-2dd2d0cf6447" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d83fc18f-3328-5871-b2d1-03511b17539e" :answer/id #uuid "d83fc18f-3328-5871-b2d1-03511b17539e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cf31f8d-0b9b-54c4-9952-e5cce2f4eb4d" :answer/id #uuid "4cf31f8d-0b9b-54c4-9952-e5cce2f4eb4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186357712 :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nTherefore, only function III could be used to represent $y(t).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-58a73fa1-b7ae-5890-859a-ca230c9a8f1d" :question/id #uuid "58a73fa1-b7ae-5890-859a-ca230c9a8f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94282" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4R\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=4R\\,⟹\\,R=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $2\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c9dae89-1437-5756-92da-8bdbfec6dd5f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c9dae89-1437-5756-92da-8bdbfec6dd5f" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c9dae89-1437-5756-92da-8bdbfec6dd5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-885afa26-b165-57dd-aac5-64a8d6e83458" :answer-field/choices ["answer-885afa26-b165-57dd-aac5-64a8d6e83458" "answer-c53a8eda-0954-5e4e-9d01-ec5263955f83" "answer-c6fde5da-39ec-5034-b103-3464e9114e0d" "answer-2069605a-a81e-57fb-a295-96c83fa94991" "answer-2c726ff3-14e5-5477-aa74-d916199a65c4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-885afa26-b165-57dd-aac5-64a8d6e83458" :answer/id #uuid "885afa26-b165-57dd-aac5-64a8d6e83458" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c53a8eda-0954-5e4e-9d01-ec5263955f83" :answer/id #uuid "c53a8eda-0954-5e4e-9d01-ec5263955f83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6fde5da-39ec-5034-b103-3464e9114e0d" :answer/id #uuid "c6fde5da-39ec-5034-b103-3464e9114e0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2069605a-a81e-57fb-a295-96c83fa94991" :answer/id #uuid "2069605a-a81e-57fb-a295-96c83fa94991" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c726ff3-14e5-5477-aa74-d916199a65c4" :answer/id #uuid "2c726ff3-14e5-5477-aa74-d916199a65c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a1d55e9e-973d-5821-a2d2-76fe96bd1d7d" :question/id #uuid "a1d55e9e-973d-5821-a2d2-76fe96bd1d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-64257" :question/problem "A particle of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=30\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+6y=0$\n\nII. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n\nIII. The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{30}{5}=6\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n1\\. $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n2\\. $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n3\\. $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{6}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 3 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5544434a-2b77-5acf-bab7-2044c21c61df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5544434a-2b77-5acf-bab7-2044c21c61df" :answer-field/id #uuid "5544434a-2b77-5acf-bab7-2044c21c61df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0572e053-bbb1-51d9-80e9-c92d6011545b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08d858c9-ca22-560b-97eb-40da3122196f" "answer-0572e053-bbb1-51d9-80e9-c92d6011545b" "answer-90dfd4e1-ed47-5aa3-9478-4f0c76e8f799" "answer-d1fd2ae2-4033-52e2-b506-d30e73572664" "answer-3dddd46e-c3a5-5c09-a4c6-1f8c377929ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08d858c9-ca22-560b-97eb-40da3122196f" :answer/id #uuid "08d858c9-ca22-560b-97eb-40da3122196f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0572e053-bbb1-51d9-80e9-c92d6011545b" :answer/id #uuid "0572e053-bbb1-51d9-80e9-c92d6011545b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90dfd4e1-ed47-5aa3-9478-4f0c76e8f799" :answer/id #uuid "90dfd4e1-ed47-5aa3-9478-4f0c76e8f799" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1fd2ae2-4033-52e2-b506-d30e73572664" :answer/id #uuid "d1fd2ae2-4033-52e2-b506-d30e73572664" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dddd46e-c3a5-5c09-a4c6-1f8c377929ed" :answer/id #uuid "3dddd46e-c3a5-5c09-a4c6-1f8c377929ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148753 :tutorial/content "**Simple harmonic motion (SHM)** is a special type of periodic motion in which an object oscillates about an equilibrium position. In SHM, the object's acceleration is proportional to the distance from the equilibrium position, and it acts in the opposite direction to the displacement.\n\nIf $y(t)$ represents the position of an object experiencing SHM, then it obeys the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nAssuming $\\omega > 0,$ the general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nFrom the above solution, we see that the **angular frequency** $\\Omega$ equals\n\n$$\n\\Omega = \\omega,\n$$\n\n and the **period** $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ represents the number of seconds it takes for the object to complete one full oscillation. Similarly, the **frequency** $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) represents the number of times the object oscillates per second and is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{1}{T} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi}.\n$$\n\nIn forms 1 and 2, the constant $R$ is called the **amplitude**, and it represents the object's maximum distance from the equilibrium position.\n\n**Note:** To see where the SHM equation comes from, remember that the object's acceleration $y''$ is proportional to the distance $y$ from the equilibrium and acts in the opposite direction as the displacement. If the constant of proportionality is $\\omega^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\ny'' = - \\omega^2 y \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147703 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
